(Filename: D:/Game Development/VoXR-Speech-Recognition/Runtime/Commands/VoxrCommandParser.cs Line: 520)

InitialiseAsync: another VoxrSpeechRecogniser (on GameObject 'OwnerRecogniser') already owns the recogniser. VoXR supports one active recogniser per process. On device the bridge is file-scope native state with no per-instance handle, so a second instance would inherit the first's model path, sample rate and gain, and free its recognizer on destroy; the Windows Editor backend is per-instance and could have coexisted, but is held to the same rule so a scene that works here cannot fail on device. Call ReleaseNativeResources() on the existing recogniser first — that frees the claim synchronously, whereas Object.Destroy only frees it at the end of the frame.
